WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.860 --> 00:00:03.490
Joe: Let’s look in the process
of an Autism Assessment.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:07.070
When an individual attends their
assessment, the assessor will

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:10.390
discuss any problems and the
interaction of the person will be

00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:12.440
watched to look for potential signs.

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:16.970
There may need to be more appointments
to follow, and those who know the

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:20.710
individual well, will be asked for any
relevant information they can provide.

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:25.290
Beth: When the individual is a
child the school, nursery or GP

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:27.040
may be asked to fill in a report.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:31.230
For a young person or adult, they may
be asked to fill in a questionnaire

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:35.380
about themselves with the GP supplying
any other medical information.

00:00:36.359 --> 00:00:40.330
At the end of the process a report
will be produced, detailing whether

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:44.600
the individual meets the criteria
for Autism spectrum diagnosis, and

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:48.190
it will give information about what
help may be needed with communication,

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:53.520
social interaction and behaviours or
sensitivity and indicate any strengths.

00:00:54.309 --> 00:00:57.370
Joe: If a formal assessment has been
done but it does not clearly show

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:01.220
whether or not they are on the spectrum,
or if someone disagrees with the

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:05.879
diagnosis, they can be referred again
or maybe have a private assessment.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:10.680
Some individuals don’t want to go through
the process of a formal diagnosis and

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:15.010
may prefer to try an online autism test,
but these cannot guarantee accuracy.

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:19.210
Beth: Informal assessments of individuals
with autism spectrum disorders

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:23.190
involve the collection of information
about the individual, interpreting

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:26.979
that information so as to better
understand the individual’s abilities.

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:32.610
Informal assessment is usually about
gaining information in order to set goals,

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:35.539
identify strategies, and measure outcomes.

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:40.149
Although the overall goals of informal
and formal assessment are similar,

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:44.430
they differ in the manner, type and
use, of the information collected.

00:01:45.179 --> 00:01:48.340
Joe: In the past formal assessment
was broken down into different

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:54.340
diagnoses such as Aspergers syndrome,
Autistic disorder, Kanner’s syndrome,

00:01:55.050 --> 00:02:00.089
childhood autism, atypical autism
and pervasive developmental disorder

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:02.609
not otherwise specified [PDD-NOS].

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:07.140
These all now come under the term
Autism spectrum disorder [ASD] or

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:09.799
Autism for short, for all diagnoses.

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:14.680
Beth: The emphasis now on diagnosis is
the specific ‘levels' of Autism spectrum

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:19.109
disorder, allowing for more clarity to be
placed on where they fit on the spectrum.

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:22.189
The levels range from
mild to severe symptoms.

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:24.730
Joe: There are three levels of autism:

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:27.230
- Level 1 requiring some support;

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.280
- Level 2 requiring substantial support

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:33.210
- Level 3 requiring very substantial support

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.940
These levels are based on a person's
strengths and limitations in regard to

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:42.829
their ability to communicate, adapt to
new situations, expand beyond restricted

00:02:42.829 --> 00:02:43.690
interests, and manage daily life.

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:48.899
Beth: Although the levels of support are
useful for indicating where an autistic

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:52.929
person falls on the spectrum regarding
severity, they have their limitations.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:58.640
They can be very subjective, for example,
some may need support at school but are

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:03.579
fine at home, while others may do well at
school but struggle in social situations.

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:08.740
It is therefore up to the clinicians to
identify the individual support needed

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:11.189
for each person that is diagnosed.

